On May 14, 2012, at 5:11 PM, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
> 1.  Ragged chains
>
> Do we let there be a different number of samples in
> each chain?  Given that this object is collecting
> samples asynchronously, it seems reasonable.
>
> But then what do we do for R-hat, ESS, etc?  The
> easiest thing to do is effectively prune down to
> the shortest chain and ignore extra samples in other
> chains.  But that makes reporting things like number
> of samples confusing and potentially error prone if
> it's different in different contexts.

I don't think I've ever seen anybody use ragged chains but I don't see a problem with them.  We could redefine R-hat, ESS, etc. to work for them.  In practice, though, I think users are going to expect chains of equal length so for now I'd recommend pruning to shortest chain.  We can see what happens in the test runs to see if it ever happens that some chains are much longer than others.

>
> 2.  Warmup samples
>
> Andrew thought we should save these.  But given that
> neither R2WinBUGS nor R2jags does this, I don't have
> a use case.  Both of those packages just returned the
> samples across all chains as an array (permuted?).
>
> If we save the warmup samples, what do we do with them?

I'd recommend we just keep them all.  We don't have to do anything special with them, the chains will just start at iteration #1.  We'll also of course save the warmup time.

> Presumably we'd prune them out of any get method for
> samples used for inference.  Or do we add a flag to
> all the get methods for +/- include warmups?

That's not an issue.  Once we want samples for inference we want random draws anyway (see below).

>
> I know we won't use them for ESS, R-hat, etc.

Right.

>
> 3.  Permutation of samples
>
> Andrew also thinks we should permute output samples.
> I'd like to know where inference will be sensitive to
> ordering and scrambling is the right thing to do.

The idea is that the user (i.e., me) doesn't want simulations organized in separate chains.  That's all just for convergece monitoring.  For example, suppose we have 4 chains, each of length 100, with 50% warmup.  Then the stan object will be 4*100*K, where K is the total number of scalar parameters (i.e., the dimension of parameter space).  But as a user, I want a 200*K array, or even more likely I want vectors of length 200 for the scalar parameters in the model, matrices of first dimension 200 for vector parameters, 3-d arrays of first dimension 200 for matrix parameters, etc.  Our extractor function should grab these.  But, right before grabbing, it should mix and scramble the sequence.  Then the user can do whatever he wants, e.g. look at one random draw of all the parameters or make a graph of a few random draws.  My point is I definitely don't want to be worrying about chains and correlations at that point.

>
> This will also complicate the interface as we'll need
> to save the permutation in order to do each parameter
> the same way.  We are not keeping the samples together,
> but rather the params, so it'll be a relatively expensive
> operation involving each sampled parameter, not just
> the iterations.

Yes, we'd save the permutation.  This shouldn't be a problem, right, because we're just mixing to take that 4*100*K array into a 200*K matrix?

>
> Also, would the permutation be within a single chain, or
> across all chains?  If the chains are different lengths
> and have warmups, what then?

In my above example, the permutation is of length 200.  It maps the 200 post-warmup draws from each the four chains concatenated, to the vector of length 200.  Ragged chains wouldn't matter; we'd just be mixing them all.

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue May 15 19:49:05 2012
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 15 May 2012 19:49:05 +0000
Subject: chains object design & permutation
Message-ID: <f5P4rTpMxPM.bafKMi1HHaUJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/f5P4rTpMxPM/m/bafKMi1HHaUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I pushed a version of mcmc::chains last night, but
given our discussion today, I'm going to need to do
some work on it before Jiqiang can use it for R or
before Daniel can add in the posterior analysis methods.

I realized this meeting that the fundamental difference
between how Andrew and I are viewing these objects is
that Andrew's looking top-down at some high-level functionality
running in the usual case, and I'm looking bottom-up at
trying to build modular components that can be independently
documented and tested and behave well on edge cases.
Of course, we want Stan to satisfy both masters -- it should
have the right top-down functionality and be modular.

Let's look at permuting outputs for scalar parameters.
I think we need three methods on the chains object:

   void
   get_warmup_samples(size_t param,
                      size_t chain,
                      vector<double>& vals);

   void
   get_kept_samples(size_t param,
                    size_t chain,
                    vector<double>& vals);

   void
   get_kept_samples(size_t param,
                    vector<double>& vals);

The first example gets warmup values (not samples) per chain
w/o scrambling.

The second gets the post-warmup samples for a chain w/o scrambling.

The third gets all the samples cross-chain with implicit
scrambling.  This will need to check if there's already an
applicable permutation of the right length and generate
one if not.  Then it needs to apply it.

But given we're permuting cross-chains into a shared chains,
applying the permutation's a bit hairy on the indexing side
if we want to avoid copies.

We probably also want versions of the above for multivariate
parameters that'll write all their values into a suitable
multi-dimensional array.

I was thinking for the middle case we'd just return
a constant reference to the actual chain, but that's not
going to work with things broken into warmup and kept.

This is with absolute indexing of parameters.  If we want
to do it by parameter name and index (e.g., entry (1,3) in matrix
"Sigma"),  then we'll use the symbol table method first to derive
the index and then call one of the above.

   size_t
   get_param_index(const string& name,
                   const vector<size_t>& indexes);

The indexing's all implemented already, but not the various
getters for warmup/non-warmup samples.

I'll work on that next.

- Bob

