On 5/15/12 5:26 PM, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> A couple quick thoughts without actually looking at what you've checked in.

Just to put this in context, this is going to
be used for the R interface, and perhaps to
aggregate multi-threaded samples in the C++
directly, but I'm worried about scaling.

> - Is a vector the most appropriate datatype for an individual chain?  Presumably this will be growing dynamically and
> requiring contiguous memory and periodic copying of larger and larger chunks of memory seems like a bad idea.  Maybe a
> deque or a list?

I don't think vector will be that much of a bottleneck.
The reassignments are fast memory local ops and the
mallocs are in blocks.  And it's the tightest way
to store and provide element-by-element access.

I just checked out how deque was implemented and
this could be the right choice (it's essentially how
we implemented our own arena-based allocator).  I had
wrongly assumed they were another kind of simple linked
list, but the doc suggests they're implemented as a hybrid
structure like database btrees.  So if we were mainly adding
an element at a time, then iterating through the
whole list, this would definitely be the way to go.
It'll take a bit more memory than std::vector and
be slower to access randomly, but much faster to reallocate.

> - One place where ragged chains may come up is parallelization across a cluster.  Some nodes of a cluster may be more
> heavily loaded, have different hardware, etc.  While less likely, this can also happen with threading depending on how
> consistent and fair the OS scheduler is.

Exactly.  We were thinking the same situation with threads
or processes -- where we run them for some fixed
amount of time.  And Matt brought up the possibility today
that adapation during warmup may go astray and cause one
chain to really lag relative to the others in efficiency
(both computational and sampling).

> - How will memory requirements scale with large numbers of iterations/parameters/chains for the computations?

O(saved-iterations * parameters * chains)

The constant's much higher for list than for deque or
vector.  It could be made tighter by managing all our
own memory arrays C-style.

Our bread and butter problems are maybe:

    1000 * 100 * 4 * 8 bytes ~ 3MB  :-)

We designed Stan around problems an order of
magnitude larger:

   1000 * 2000 * 4 * 8 bytes

I'm interested in problems that are more like

   1000 * 20000 * 4 * 8 bytes

but that still hasn't even taken us to a GB yet!

Beyond that, I'm not sure you're going to be running
through R anyway.

> - With grouping by individial parameters (instead of grouping by samples) how will fetching multivariate parameters
> work?  E.g., how do I get back samples of a coviariance matrix?  It seems strangely complicated to have to get back
> samples of each entry separately.

There's a parallel track of parameters organized by name.
The C++ code will implement the following

   template <typename T>
   void get_parameter(size_t param_index,
                      size_t chain_index,
                      T& vals);

So T might be an Eigen matrix.  There are also methods
to get the components, as in

   double get_parameter(size_t param_index,
                        const vector<size_t>& indexes,
                        size_t chain_index);

where the indexes might be something like (1,2) for
getting element (1,2) from a matrix.  This is really
clunky in C++.

Yes.  All part of Stan's transform machinery (you
see it at the top of the generated log_prob functions
in the generated model C++ code).

- Bob

